WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.210
It's great to welcome to the program today.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:08.210
Edward Watts, who is both professor and chair
in the history department at the University

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.349
of California, San Diego.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:15.599
He is also author of the book Mortal Republic
How Rome Fell into Tyranny, as well as the

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:21.340
upcoming book Rome's Eternal Decline and Fall,
available next year from Oxford University

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.340
Press.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.420
It's so great to have you on, Edward.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.490
Thank you, David.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.060
I'm really excited to be here.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:31.080
So increasingly, I'm hearing things from my
audience about, listen.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.920
All great empires have fallen in history.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.450
Sometimes they fall slowly.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:44.250
The idea of the United States as this permanent
empire is a historical and you wouldn't even

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.570
necessarily know it while it's happening.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:52.060
So maybe as an analogous situation, we can
start with with Rome.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.170
Talk a little bit about how did Rome fall?

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.510
How long did it take and how noticeable was
it at the time?

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:03.680
Yeah, I mean, the interesting thing about
Rome is the transition from the republic to

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.750
the Empire is something that lasts nearly
100 years.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.800
And it starts with a series of problems mainly
connected to wealth inequality that you see

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.960
emerging with the development of a financial
system in the second century B.C. and the

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.260
initials Faizan.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.470
This was one where people were concerned about
the issue, but trusted the system to resolve

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.470
it.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:31.270
And after about a generation, it became clear
the system was not effectively resolving this

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.770
issue.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.070
And you started getting political conflict,
but it took another century before the system

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.560
completely collapsed.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:44.110
And I think what you see in that is the system
continually reset itself over that time so

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.480
that there would be a crisis, people would
feel like the crisis passed and they would

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.370
assume that that was the end of it.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:55.000
And this long, durable and really successful
Republican system had weathered the crisis

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.120
and would move on.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:06.190
So at the time, if you were an average Roman
citizen and you recognized that maybe your

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:11.140
life wasn't that good, you saw inequality
much the way that inequality, inequality is

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.160
noticeable today.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.120
You would not necessarily have said this.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.560
This is part of the eventual downfall.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.740
I think that that's right.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.100
I think that when you're in a successful political
system that's been around for a very long

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.100
time.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.700
The assumption is that that system has something
natural that makes it succeed.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.870
And you don't assume that that system is flawed
or that system might be undergoing changes

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.060
that could completely undermine it.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.040
And the other thing that's important is in
the Roman context, these crises would happen

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.040
maybe once a generation.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.820
And so you would live through it and you would
assume that was that was bad.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.790
But now things are back to normal and we can
move forward.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:03:01.350
And it's only with the ability to see more
than a centuries sequence of change that we

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.180
can say, well, this is actually a very long
process of degeneration and decline.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.250
But the people living through it didn't live
through that century.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.940
And when they did experience these things,
they experienced them in real time.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.170
And so something that happened 25 years ago
will feel very distant, even though as a historian,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.099
we look at this and we say this is part of
a century long process.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:25.900
And I think in the U.S., we can see something
very similar.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.410
When did this process of political degeneration
start in the United States?

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.410
Did it start in the 60s?

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:32.820
Did it start in the 90s?

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.280
Did it start in 2016?

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.890
But we continually think these things reset.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:43.870
And the reality is, of course, we might be
part of a process that started 60 years ago

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.629
and we just haven't noticed that we're in
the middle of something that will last a long

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:48.629
time.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:52.900
This analogy, this idea is used very often
by technologists and futurists, which which,

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:59.700
you know, they they point out that when you
are in the middle of rapid technological change

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:04.780
on a day to day basis, it really doesn't feel
very fast then because of our lifespans and

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:10.390
the ways in which we interact with the world,
with technology is advancing more quickly

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:11.390
or less quickly.

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:14.441
It's not often that noticeable as you're living
through it.

00:04:14.441 --> 00:04:17.709
And it seems that you're pointing out something
similar.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:20.049
I think that that's exactly right.

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:26.440
We experience everything on a human level
in a day is 24 hours, just like it was in

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.729
the Roman period.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:29.639
And so that means that change evolves.

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:33.270
It can evolve very rapidly or it can take
a long time.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.419
And if it's taking a long time, we aren't
always noticing that process because our attention

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:40.629
shifts to something else.

00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:45.530
And so I think the technological analogy is
a very good one, because we live with this

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:46.810
on a daily basis.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:47.810
We experience it.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.150
We interact with it.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:54.490
And so the changes are things that we don't
necessarily recognize as part of a long process

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:00.009
that last decades or even a century when we
think about the United States.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:07.509
Now, as with 330 million people, one of the
most populous countries in the world, although

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:11.330
certainly not not the most anymore.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:18.469
Is there some historical analogy in terms
of the idea of the country separating into

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:20.110
smaller countries?

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:25.120
And this is something that my audience asks
frequently, typically for cultural reasons,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.169
in the sense that, you know, when you look
at the Northeast compared to most of the states

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:34.189
in the mountain time zone, there are not only
there, not only very far when you look at,

00:05:34.189 --> 00:05:38.289
you know, a continent the size of Europe,
these would be many, many countries away.

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:44.409
Culturally, there are significant differences
religiously when it comes to economics.

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:49.639
Is there any historical analogy we can look
at for the successful successful being the

00:05:49.639 --> 00:05:55.680
key piece sort of sort of this assembly of
a larger country into component parts, or

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.280
is that something that historically has not
ever gone well?

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.460
The Roman Empire actually did this.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:07.210
So in the later 4th century, the Roman Empire
devolved into a system of kind of central

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.419
administration was shared responsibility.

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:13.059
So empires were based in the West and based
in the east.

00:06:13.059 --> 00:06:17.020
And generally speaking, that worked pretty
well in the fourth century.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:22.930
But in 395, those territories separated and
generally speaking, that separation was something

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.889
that people acknowledged had happened.

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:29.899
But there was this understanding that the
east and the West were one imperial system.

00:06:29.899 --> 00:06:33.349
Laws issued in the east were binding on the
West.

00:06:33.349 --> 00:06:34.930
But eventually that stopped.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:41.669
And as you move into the sixth century, there's
a Roman regime based in Italy that is effectively

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:43.669
separate from the east.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:48.330
And there were conflicts eventually that developed,
but much longer.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.099
It's a much longer process.

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:55.400
And the conflicts developed after almost a
half century of these sort of interactions

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.089
being peaceful and more or less.

00:06:58.089 --> 00:06:59.089
Respectful.

00:06:59.089 --> 00:07:04.509
And so you can see this, but I think the process
in Rome was one where the needs of the individual

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:10.749
regions were such that the central administration
couldn't effectively address all of the problems

00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:12.949
across this vast territory.

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:16.550
And the Roman Empire was bigger than the United
States at that point.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:20.960
And so I think that that would be the example
we might look at as you if you have different

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:26.499
regions with different needs, a central government
needs to be able to fairly address those challenges.

00:07:26.499 --> 00:07:32.619
And if it can't, then there really is a need
for something that does more effectively address

00:07:32.619 --> 00:07:37.099
regional challenges then a central administration
is capable of doing.

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:41.740
There are going to be people who listen to
this conversation and say that even though

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:48.080
in terms of the number of years in in terms
of sort of human history, the last few hundred

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.509
years of the United States are, ah, it's a
short period of time that such significant

00:07:53.509 --> 00:07:58.889
changes have taken place technologically with
regard to sort of the state of homosapiens

00:07:58.889 --> 00:08:05.809
on this planet, that looking at the fall of
Rome is simply not analogous to what we have

00:08:05.809 --> 00:08:12.990
today, that we have, you know, of government
systems that are more developed with all sorts

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:17.749
of different mechanisms from executive orders,
the ability to print money, currency, treasury

00:08:17.749 --> 00:08:23.240
interest did the full list of differences
and that what happened then could not happen

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.569
now in the same way.

00:08:25.569 --> 00:08:27.899
What would you say to people who would assert
that?

00:08:27.899 --> 00:08:31.800
I would say on some level that's correct.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.390
On another level, I think we underestimate
what the Romans were able to do.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.390
OK.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:37.390
I'm sorry.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:38.390
I would say that that's correct.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.460
In you know, in large part because you're
exactly right.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:46.470
The technological sophistication, the communication
mechanisms, the extent of what government

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:47.470
does.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.470
All of these things.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.470
Romans couldn't have even imagined.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:54.300
You know, that's an empire that was governed
based on sending messages as fast as someone

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.640
could carry them.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:00.250
And so you had a system that was based on
like it taking two weeks for you to hear what

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:02.880
happens in Egypt when you're based in Rome.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.470
And so that's exactly right.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.200
That there are really significant differences.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.460
But there's also elements of how polities
come together and how people develop expectations

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:16.140
of what it means to be part of a political
system.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.440
And in that sense, Rome can help us think
about some of the dynamics that we experience

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.440
and we see.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:27.910
And so Rome, I think, offers us a lot of tools
to think with and to imagine what possibilities

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:30.980
might come out of the political challenges
we face.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:32.740
I mean, so I agree with you.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:40.680
Rome is not it's not the United States 1.0,
but Rome does offer us ways to think in in

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:47.750
a new manner about what could happen here
and to imagine possibilities that maybe are

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:53.300
most recent and maybe even the American historical
experience doesn't allow for us simply because

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.710
this historical experience is not long enough
to show some of these possibilities.

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:02.910
I wonder if another difference or I wonder
how relevant another difference might be that

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:10.950
the power of militaries and law enforcement
today in many countries is very different

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:21.430
in terms of both its capacity to repress certain
types of of of uprising as well as its centralization,

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:27.940
combined with the rapid spread of, you know,
you the when the George Floyd protests started,

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:32.470
they they almost started in tandem in cities
all across the country.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:37.630
But they were really discreetly, separately
organized protests thanks to technology and

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.780
communication methods, something that, as
you point out, would have been very different

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.630
some time ago.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.460
What do you think about that?

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.570
As you know, the power of military and law
enforcement in most Western democracies now

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:55.570
as something that would maybe prevent circumstances
from getting to a certain point.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:11:02.720
I think that the thing this is a lesson from
Rome that I think is important to understand.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.030
In Rome, you had say in the fourth century,
you had about 60 million people and perhaps

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.150
500000 soldiers.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:12.680
And so it was a very heavily militarized society.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:18.350
And the way that that society worked and the
way military was used to quell dissent was

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.820
symbolic shows of force.

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:20.820
Right.

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.690
You couldn't actually police this entire empire,
even with 500000 troops.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:31.400
And so what you would do is you would basically
deploy troops to demonstrate that order and

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:37.770
law were still in place and that anarchy had
not kind of developed and that there wasn't

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:41.710
a kind of separation from the empire in a
particular region.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:46.700
And I think that this is this is still a feature
of modern Western democracies.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:47.700
Right.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:54.320
I mean, law enforcement needs to law enforcement
does not have the capacity to be everywhere

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:55.930
at all times.

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:01.850
But I think that you're right, that one thing
that is different now is the the spread, the

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:07.420
rapid spread of protests and reactions to
stimuli.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:11.370
This is something that, of course, couldn't
happen in the Roman Empire, because if something

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:18.330
happened in, say, Antioch in Syria, no one
in Italy would know about it for two weeks.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.850
And so you wouldn't have had a spontaneous
protest that happened everywhere immediately

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:27.370
in response to something because news wouldn't
travel fast enough.

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:32.570
And so I think there you do have a very significant
difference that that presents a real challenge

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:37.920
administratively for a central government
that the Romans never had to deal with when

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.420
people were thinking now about 2020, when
people point to what they have observed in

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:50.210
the last four years of this administration
and then make can draw conclusions from that

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:54.880
about the direction of democracy or the state
of.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:13:02.790
Of the United States is for years, even theoretically,
a long enough period of time to actually identify

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:09.540
significant changes in the state of democracy
or or is it simply too short a period of time?

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:14.000
I think we can see really significant changes
in the way that we do things and talk about

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.110
things in this country in the last four years.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:22.060
The thing that's particularly alarming to
me is the rise of violent rhetoric and then

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:27.290
the fact that we now have violence spilling
out into the streets and in particular the

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:28.290
way that the.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:33.910
Rhetoric that's coming from the White House
is matched by armed people going into the

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:37.250
streets to back up this way of thinking.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:41.870
This is really different than anything we've
seen before in the United States, certainly

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:44.460
in the last 50 years.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:50.310
And this is something that when this emerged
in Rome, that was impossible for the republic

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:51.720
to control.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:57.410
There's a basic idea in any republic, I think,
but it's articulated very well by Romans that

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:03.070
a republic that is governed by law can be
it can endure forever in theory, but a republic

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:07.210
that's governed by violence is not a republic
at all because there's no consensus between

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:12.510
the people ruling and the people being ruled
that this is a lawful way of behaving.

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:18.180
And so violence, once it emerges into political
discourse, discussion and political debate,

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:23.980
is something that completely corrodes the
lawful ability for our society to make decisions.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:28.010
And so I think that is something that's happened
in the last four years that is really, profoundly

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:31.000
dangerous and really alarming.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:35.590
What should we be paying particular attention
to over the next?

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:41.930
For example, six weeks in terms of the election,
three months in terms of the transition period

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:47.100
and then the following four year presidential
term that will come after that?

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:52.130
I think that if there's anything that we need
to be particularly vigilant in controlling,

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.870
it's getting violence out of our political
sphere.

00:14:55.870 --> 00:15:00.790
People protesting with weapons is not something
that democracies should be willing to tolerate.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:03.520
It's really, really dangerous.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:08.800
And the other thing that that you see in the
Roman period is when you start with that very

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.400
quickly, you start getting martyrs.

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:15.500
You know, people who are protesting with weapons
may not actually intend to hurt anybody.

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:21.060
It may just be sort of a symbolic display
of the capacity to be violent if necessary.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.120
But protests are heated.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.670
And when there's a political discussion and
people are armed, sometimes there is violence

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.630
that no one intended.

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:33.360
But as soon as you have martyrs, it's very,
very hard to walk back from that.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:38.180
And it's very, very hard to remove violence
from a political environment when you have

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:40.770
people who have died for a cause.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:44.610
And so this is, you know, as especially as
we're looking at the next six weeks and maybe

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:48.110
the next few months, we need to walk back
from that.

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:53.050
Everybody needs to walk back from that, because
if you do have the eruption of political violence

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:58.130
in the context of this election, it's going
to be very, very hard to control that and

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:00.310
very, very hard to get that out of the way.

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:02.190
We make political decisions in the future.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:08.540
Yeah, it's a bell that's difficult to unring
once, once, once you've rung it.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:13.790
We've been speaking with Edward Watts, who
is both professor of and chair of the history

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:15.380
department at UC San Diego.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:22.089
The upcoming book is Rome's Eternal Decline
and Fall due out next year.

00:16:22.089 --> 00:16:24.550
Really great having you on and I so appreciate
your time.

